<p>There are 18 English marathon plans in the catalog. That sounds like a lot, but they're really just four decisions. Answer them in order and you'll land on one.</p>

<p>The second question is the one that matters most, and it's the one most runners skip: <strong>don't choose by goal time, choose by the volume you're running now</strong>. A plan built around 110 km weeks won't make you faster if you're coming off 50. It'll injure you in week six.</p>

<h2>1. How many weeks until race day?</h2>

<p>Two options, and the calendar usually decides for you:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>18 weeks</strong> — if you have four months or more. This is the full block: base, build, peak and taper, with enough slack to lose a week to illness or travel without losing the plan.</li>
  <li><strong>12 weeks</strong> — if the race is three months out. It works, but it assumes you're already running consistently. It is not a plan for starting from zero.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you're inside 12 weeks and not currently training, this isn't the race for this cycle. That's the least popular conversation I have with athletes and the one that prevents the most injuries.</p>

<h2>2. What weekly volume are you actually running?</h2>

<p>This defines everything else. The plans split into three levels by weekly volume, not by ambition:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Level</th><th>Weekly volume</th><th>Who it's for</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Base</strong></td><td>under 90 km / 55 mi</td><td>First marathon, or returning after a break</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Build</strong></td><td>90–110 km / 55–68 mi</td><td>You've run a marathon and want a faster one</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Peak</strong></td><td>over 110 km / 68 mi</td><td>Experienced runner chasing a personal best</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="datanote">
  <p><strong>What that costs you in actual hours.</strong> These come from the structured workouts inside the plans themselves, not from an estimate:</p>
  <p><strong>Base</strong> ≈ 6 h/week · <strong>Build</strong> ≈ 8 h/week · <strong>Peak</strong> ≈ 9 h/week (median across 23 marathon plans).</p>
  <p>If you look at your week and can't find 8 hours, don't buy a Build plan. The gap between Base and Peak is about 3 hours a week for four months — roughly 50 hours. It's a scheduling decision as much as a fitness one.</p>
</div>

<h2>3. What do you train by?</h2>

<p>The same plan exists in different versions depending on what you use to control intensity. This isn't a detail — it's what makes a plan executable rather than frustrating.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Heart rate</strong> — if you have a watch with a monitor and nothing else. The default, and the most robust.</li>
  <li><strong>Pace</strong> — if you run mostly flat terrain and know your times. More direct, but heat, wind and hills all lie to you.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Do you want strength work included?</h2>

<p>Several plans come in a version with a gym routine built into the week. Strength isn't optional for a marathon — it's what holds your form together when fatigue arrives at mile 20. The only good reason to take the running-only version is that you already have a strength routine you don't want to disturb.</p>

